The important conceptual distinction is that your system is not merely trying to create another programming-language syntax. It is moving toward a semantic execution language.

A conventional language primarily expresses operations:

x = calculate(a, b)

Your Q model is intended to express the semantic lifecycle surrounding an operation:

^↑D
    detect
    analyze
    infer
    classify
    register
    learn
^D
    create
    validate
^|D
    execute

That makes ^↑D fundamentally different from ^D and ^|D.

^↑D is the knowledge/understanding phase. It establishes what something is, what relationships exist, what can be inferred, and what knowledge should be retained.

^D is the construction phase. It turns the resulting semantic understanding into an artifact, model, protocol, program, object, plan, or other construct and validates it before execution.

^|D is the execution phase. It crosses the boundary from semantic description into actual runtime effects.

That separation is particularly important for LMLM because it gives the runtime an opportunity to verify an intended action before allowing it to produce side effects.

The resulting architecture can therefore be expressed as:

                    Q-SCRIPT
                       │
                       ▼
                 Semantic Parser
                       │
                       ▼
                Semantic AST / IR
                       │
             ┌─────────┴─────────┐
             │                   │
           ^↑D                  ^D
             │                   │
       understand             construct
             │                   │
             └─────────┬─────────┘
                       ▼
                  ^|D execute
                       │
                       ▼
                 LMLM Coordinator
                       │
              ROUTE → INSTRUCT
                       │
                    VERIFY
                       │
                    RESULT
                       │
          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
          ▼            ▼            ▼
        Model         Tool          API

This also clarifies where .qs should sit. .qs should be the source representation, not the runtime itself.

In other words:

Q-lang       = language/semantic model
.qs          = Q Semantic Script source
Q AST/IR     = machine representation
Q Runtime    = execution machinery
LMLM         = multimodal orchestration layer
GOD Protocol = runtime coordination protocol

I would therefore avoid prematurely defining .qs as “Qt Script.” In your architecture it has a completely different purpose.

